# wpa_supplicant -- the 802.11 supplicant, and the reason the Wi-Fi panel does
# anything at all.
#
# NetworkManager 1.54.0 contains no 802.11 authentication code. It scans,
# associates and authenticates by driving wpa_supplicant over D-Bus
# (fi.w1.wpa_supplicant1), which is why this package matters far more than its
# size suggests: without it NetworkManager starts, the Wi-Fi panel loads, and
# every network fails to connect with nothing in the UI to say why. Compare
# pkgs/libnl/pkg.env, which explains that NetworkManager does NOT use libnl --
# wpa_supplicant is the package that does.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env -- versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh.
SRC_URL=https://w1.fi/releases/wpa_supplicant-2.11.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=912ea06f74e30a8e36fbb68064d6cdff218d8d591db0fc5d75dee6c81ac7fc0a
SRC_DIR=wpa_supplicant-2.11

# THERE IS NO configure HERE. wpa_supplicant is built from a hand-written
# .config read by ../src/build.rules, and the build happens in the
# wpa_supplicant/ subdirectory rather than at the top of the tree. Both the
# build and the install stage are therefore this recipe's own -- see build.sh,
# which writes that file and explains every line in it, and install.sh, which
# installs the two D-Bus files `make install` does not.
#
# THE TRAP THAT SHAPES THIS RECIPE, and the reason build.sh is as commented as
# it is: an unrecognised CONFIG_ line in .config is SILENTLY IGNORED. There is
# no unknown-symbol warning and no error -- the Makefile simply never tests a
# variable nobody defined, and the build succeeds without the feature. That is
# not hypothetical: CONFIG_PEERKEY appears in the wpa_supplicant configuration
# every distribution copies from, and it was REMOVED from the build system
# upstream. In 2.11 the string does not occur in the Makefile or in
# drivers.mak at all, so every tree still carrying that line is carrying a
# comment that looks like a setting. Checked by grepping the tarball rather
# than by trusting the config everyone shares.
#
# So the flags here cannot be verified by reading the config back -- it will
# read back exactly as written whether or not anything used it. They are
# verified in post-install.sh, against the built binaries.

# WHERE THE BINARIES GO, and it has to be here rather than in install.sh
# because BOTH STAGES NEED THE SAME VALUE.
#
# Upstream defaults BINDIR to /usr/local/sbin (wpa_supplicant/Makefile line 46).
# Two different things consume it, at two different times:
#
#   the BUILD stage    generates dbus/fi.w1.wpa_supplicant1.service from its
#                      .in by substituting @BINDIR@ (the `%.service: %.service.in`
#                      rule), so the activation file's Exec= line is baked at
#                      COMPILE time;
#   the INSTALL stage  copies the binaries to $(DESTDIR)$(BINDIR).
#
# Passing it only to `make install` -- which is the obvious thing to do, and
# what this recipe did first -- puts the binaries in /usr/sbin and leaves the
# activation file pointing at /usr/local/sbin. make then reports "Nothing to be
# made" for the service file, because it was already generated and is newer
# than its source. The result installs cleanly, passes every path check, and
# produces a daemon dbus-daemon cannot start: NetworkManager asks for
# fi.w1.wpa_supplicant1, dbus-daemon runs /usr/local/sbin/wpa_supplicant, and
# nothing is there.
#
# Caught by post-install.sh, which asserts the path INSIDE the file rather than
# the file's existence. That is the whole of finding 8 in one line: the file
# existing is the input, the path it contains is the outcome.
#
# /usr/sbin is a real directory here -- duct-filesystem makes usr/bin, usr/sbin
# and usr/lib real and points the top-level /bin, /sbin, /lib at them -- so
# this stages safely. /sbin would be the mistake.
WPA_BINDIR=/usr/sbin
